The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
np = of_find_compatible_node(NULL, NULL, "fsl,pq1-pic");
if (np == NULL) {
    printk(KERN_ERR "Could not find fsl,pq1-pic node\n");
    return -ENOMEM;
}
```
Reason:
The function `of_find_compatible_node()` increments the reference count of the device node it returns. If the function fails to find a compatible node and returns `NULL`, the code exits without decrementing the reference count, leading to a potential memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
of_node_put(np);
```
Reason:
The addition of `of_node_put(np);` in the `out` label ensures that the reference count of the device node is decremented before the function exits, preventing the memory leak that occurs when the reference is not released.

